48:24.873 --> 48:28.797
[SPEAKER_00]: This thing I don't see so much in these days with other Lamas.

48:29.357 --> 48:34.423
[SPEAKER_00]: Either you follow him and be his lineage, you can't be from a different lineage and be his student.

48:35.123 --> 48:41.010
[SPEAKER_00]: This, I think, is like one unique quality of being a real spiritual releme master.

48:41.090 --> 48:41.430
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

48:41.931 --> 48:48.498
[SPEAKER_00]: And one, even I found, after Khyentse Rinpoche passed away, one letter he wrote to a pen practitioner.

48:48.598 --> 48:48.878
[SPEAKER_00]: Wow.

48:49.679 --> 48:52.501
[SPEAKER_00]: and teaching him how to practice according to burn.

48:53.261 --> 49:01.105
[SPEAKER_00]: So this kind of openness is something very special quality of Khyentse Rinpoche.

49:01.445 --> 49:13.412
[SPEAKER_01]: So he was a teacher across traditions and he can really influence people, but they didn't feel like they then had to become Nyingma or from Xie Xian, this type of thing.

49:14.232 --> 49:18.655
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think this is an important point because when you read the Namtas of the great masters,

49:19.595 --> 49:22.858
[SPEAKER_01]: You see that they had many teachers from many different traditions.

49:23.198 --> 49:26.320
[SPEAKER_00]: Khyentse Rinpoche had 50 different teachers from all the different schools.

49:26.420 --> 49:26.821
[SPEAKER_00]: Wow.

49:27.461 --> 49:35.447
[SPEAKER_00]: And three men from Gelug, I think the 14th Dalai Lama, in the number eight pension, and one Gelug Geshe from Amdo.

49:36.728 --> 49:44.591
[SPEAKER_00]: One, actually, the Gelung Geshe, he's the one who predicted that Khyentse Rinpoche's Dharma activity will cover the one third of the world or something like that.

49:44.611 --> 49:45.252
[SPEAKER_00]: One third of the world?

49:45.292 --> 49:46.112
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, something like that.

49:46.132 --> 49:47.813
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I think this is coming true, right?

49:48.113 --> 49:48.373
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

49:49.133 --> 49:54.516
[SPEAKER_01]: And then also, if you look at his Holiness Dalai Lama, Khyentse Rinpoche was a teacher to his Holiness.

49:54.536 --> 50:02.380
[SPEAKER_00]: His Holiness Dalai Lama mentioned once, I heard in Drepung, that he really learned how the Rime is practiced from Khyentse Rinpoche.

50:03.460 --> 50:04.561
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's, yes.

50:05.177 --> 50:11.882
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so these great masters can draw from the great teachers no matter what tradition.

50:11.902 --> 50:12.583
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes.

50:13.223 --> 50:22.170
[SPEAKER_01]: And does this extend like even into like the secular world, like teaching scientists, teaching... Because nowadays, you know, mindfulness is very prominent in the West.

50:22.210 --> 50:24.913
[SPEAKER_01]: I haven't fully formed the question, but people are learning mindfulness.

50:25.893 --> 50:28.836
[SPEAKER_01]: But normally we talk about view, meditation and conduct.

50:29.596 --> 50:38.139
[SPEAKER_01]: And so do you think when they're just, the mindfulness is so popular, is it, it seems to be missing view, but is that okay?

50:38.379 --> 50:41.720
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness maybe can act like a door to a dhamma.

50:42.020 --> 50:42.240
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

50:42.720 --> 50:47.082
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, people get more interested in mindfulness and somehow it benefits them.

50:47.482 --> 50:49.963
[SPEAKER_00]: Then they get more interested to go into deeper practice.

50:50.223 --> 50:50.423
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

50:50.843 --> 50:54.244
[SPEAKER_00]: So because people a little bit hesitation with religion.

50:54.625 --> 50:54.865
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

50:55.165 --> 51:04.248
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's not mindfulness can be like yoga was at one point very popular and many people through yoga they somehow get deeper into practice.

51:05.129 --> 51:10.971
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think whatever benefits beings I think that is the manifestation of Buddha Dharma.

51:11.271 --> 51:11.491
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

51:12.258 --> 51:14.619
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I think the mindfulness can become a door.

51:14.659 --> 51:17.239
[SPEAKER_00]: We cannot say the mindfulness is the ultimate practice.

51:17.299 --> 51:17.959
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

51:17.999 --> 51:19.200
[SPEAKER_00]: But it can be like a door.

51:19.620 --> 51:19.820
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

51:20.280 --> 51:27.622
[SPEAKER_00]: And then those from which then people who want to go deeper into practice, then I'm sure then they will have the full practice.

51:27.822 --> 51:28.062
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

51:28.242 --> 51:36.924
[SPEAKER_01]: And what if someone is doing meditation, mindfulness, what is their next step if they want to go deeper and enter one of those doors?

51:37.344 --> 51:54.955
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think once they get into the kind of, let's say, the taste of the mindfulness, then I think they will have to really get into serious.

51:55.515 --> 52:00.378
[SPEAKER_00]: Normally, mindfulness is more like a shinai, isn't it?

52:00.658 --> 52:01.559
[SPEAKER_00]: More like a shamatha.

52:02.119 --> 52:03.820
[SPEAKER_00]: So shamatha can always be like a door.

52:05.557 --> 52:11.302
[SPEAKER_00]: So if they want to get deeper into it, then they'll have to take refuge in, you know, start from the control.

52:11.602 --> 52:12.343
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

52:12.463 --> 52:13.904
[SPEAKER_01]: So finding a teacher is important.

52:13.924 --> 52:14.584
[SPEAKER_01]: Finding a teacher.

52:14.965 --> 52:20.909
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, thank you so much for joining us and being so generous with your time, but also so generous with your stories.

52:20.930 --> 52:21.770
[SPEAKER_01]: That's very precious.

52:21.830 --> 52:22.451
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you so much.

52:22.651 --> 52:22.971
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

52:22.991 --> 52:23.552
[SPEAKER_01]: So wonderful.

52:23.572 --> 52:23.892
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

52:31.348 --> 52:41.916
[SPEAKER_01]: Next on the Wisdom Podcast, I speak with Tara Brach, American psychologist, renowned meditation teacher, and author of many books, including the bestseller, Radical Acceptance, Embracing Your Life with the Heart of a Buddha.

52:42.717 --> 52:48.301
[SPEAKER_01]: Tara is widely celebrated for her pioneering work combining Western psychology and Eastern spiritual practices.

52:49.242 --> 52:51.064
[SPEAKER_01]: And it comes from science, Daniel.

52:51.144 --> 52:56.528
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like there's so much research now on the difference between states and traits that

52:57.115 --> 53:11.221
[SPEAKER_01]: And if we touch after we nurture, this is generally, there's some more spaciousness and tenderness, but if we rush right on, we don't end up getting familiar in a way that we have more access to it.

53:11.921 --> 53:13.802
[SPEAKER_01]: So what neuroscience is showing

53:14.622 --> 53:18.688
[SPEAKER_01]: If we have a negative experience, it's very sticky and it stays in our unconscious.

53:18.748 --> 53:20.791
[SPEAKER_01]: Positive ones, not so much.

53:20.872 --> 53:27.461
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that thing that Rick Hansen always says is that, you know, we're Velcro for negative experiences and Teflon for the positive.

53:27.982 --> 53:29.585
